{"resourceIdentifier":"3786","resourceName":"Silvopastoral carbon project of Uruguay","description":"This grouped project consists of afforestation and reforestation activities, with an innovative silvopastoral system design taking place in Uruguay. The project activity involves the inclusion of exotic and native trees on grassland, which is in a degraded state due to a long history of overgrazing. Trees are introduced at a low density with a unique plantation frame so that both livestock grazing and forestry activities are integrated without displacing any previous activity. Some instances also include a novel intensive grazing management. \r\nThe main aim is to sequester atmospheric carbon through photosynthesis, to be stored in aboveground and belowground tree biomass, as well as dead wood, litter, and soil organic carbon pools. The most common tree species will be Eucalyptus grandis (W.Hill), however alternative species including various native species will be included as part of the project.\r\nThis system is an alternative to the conventional high-density, large-scale plantations for pulp or sawn wood that are the most common forestry activities in the country. Despite the numerous barriers for its implementation, management, and harvesting, it is an option for farmers who want to include trees as shelter and shadow without leaving behind animal husbandry and replacing the native grass cover. Because of the low volume of production, normally the labor during planting and forest management is done by local people and the timber is sold to nearby small-scale sawmills, contributing to the local communities by providing a source of qualified jobs in the primary and secondary sectors. \r\nThis project does not only contribute to the local and regional economy but also has a net positive impact on social and environmental aspects. With the utilization of native species, new job positions will be created in existing or new-to-exist nurseries, among other social benefits.","location":{"latitude":-31.925178,"longitude":-56.038017},"inPublicCommentPeriod":false,"lastPublicCommentPeriod":{"startInclusive":"2023-04-21","endExclusive":"2023-05-22","endInclusive":"2023-05-21"},"attributes":[{"code":"PROJECT_ID","values":[{"type":"string","value":"3786"}]},{"code":"STATE_PROVINCE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Tacuarembó and Cerro Largo"}]}],"participationSummaries":[{"programCode":"VCS","attributes":[{"code":"PROPONENT_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Multiple Proponents"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_STATUS","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Registered"},{"type":"assetSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"3786"},{"type":"bufferSearchLink","value":null,"targetId":"3786"}]},{"code":"EST_ANNUAL_EMISSION_REDCT","values":[{"type":"string","value":"405"}]},{"code":"TOTAL_BUFFER_POOL_CREDITS","values":[{"type":"integer","value":35}]},{"code":"PRIMARY_PROJECT_CATEGORY_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"Agriculture Forestry and Other Land Use"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_SUBCATERGORY_NAMES","values":[{"type":"string","value":"ARR"}]},{"code":"PROTOCOL_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"AR-ACM0003"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_ACREAGE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"944 Hectares"}]},{"code":"VALIDATOR_NAME","values":[{"type":"string","value":"AENOR CONFIA, S.A. (UNIPERSONAL)"}]},{"code":"PROJECT_REGISTRATION_DATE","values":[{"type":"string","value":"01/09/2025"}]},{"code":"CREDIT_PERIOD_INFO","values":[{"type":"string","value":"1st, 28/10/2019 - 27/10/2119"}]}]}],"documentGroups":[{"code":"VCS_PIPELINE_DOCUMENTS","description":"VCS Pipeline Documents","documents":[{"uri":"https://registry.verra.org/mymodule/ProjectDoc/Project_ViewFile.asp?FileID=74918&IDKEY=7097809fdslkjf09rndasfufd098asodfjlkduf09nm23mrn87r103311922","documentType":"Draft Project Description","documentName":"VCS-Project-Description_Silvopastoral Project of Uruguay_under development.pdf","uploadDate":"2022-09-28T20:57:48.097Z"},{"uri":"https://registry.verra.org/mymodu